\import{fdip-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}
\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Lemma}
\title{The determinant of a rank-one update}
\date{2026-11-21}

\p{Let #{x} be a column and #{y^{\top}} a row, with entries in a
commutative unital ring. Then
##{\det({\bbI}-xy^{\top})=1-y^{\top}x.}
If #{A} is an invertible square matrix of the same size, and
#{p} and #{c} are columns,
##{\det(A-pc^{\top})=\det(A)\bigl(1-c^{\top}A^{-1}p\bigr).}}

\proof{
\p{Form the block matrix with upper blocks #{\bbI,x} and lower
blocks #{y^{\top},1}. Adding a scalar multiple of one column to a
different column does not change the determinant. The determinant
is the alternating sum of permutation products. The added terms
repeat a column, and transposing those two columns negates the
sum while leaving the matrix unchanged, so those terms add
nothing. The same holds for rows. The column operations clear the
upper part of the last column and leave the corner
#{1-y^{\top}x}. The row operations, applied instead to the
original block matrix, clear the upper right block and leave
#{{\bbI}-xy^{\top}} in the upper left. In each reduced matrix
the last column has only its bottom entry nonzero, so every
surviving permutation product uses that entry. The determinant
equals that entry times the determinant of the complementary
block. One complementary block is the identity and the other is
#{{\bbI}-xy^{\top}}. Thus
#{\det({\bbI}-xy^{\top})=1-y^{\top}x}. For invertible #{A},
##{A-pc^{\top}=A({\bbI}-A^{-1}pc^{\top}),}
and the identity already proved supplies the second factor.}
}
